---
/**
 * Bring the agents you already use.
 *
 * The six cards are gone. Five of them repeated the marks that now float in the
 * middle of the section, and explained brands the reader already knows; the
 * sixth was a text card wedged into a logo grid. Everything true and load-
 * bearing in them survives in the panel copy below, so nothing was lost except
 * repetition:
 *  - Native is built in and reaches ELEVEN providers with one key
 *    (nativeProviders.ts:20-53 is seven OpenAI-compatible plus four built-in).
 *  - Agents coordinate through the board and structured messages, never by
 *    reading each other's terminal output (codexDriver.ts:1-4).
 *  - Each works in its own copy of the code, so two cannot overwrite each other.
 *  - The runtimes guide link.
 *
 * ONE CAVEAT, deliberately accepted: "everyone knows these logos" holds for
 * Claude Code and Codex, less so for Hermes and OpenClaw, and not at all for
 * Clawboo Native, which is this project's own mascot AND the one carrying the
 * eleven-providers story. That is why the panel copy leads with Native by name
 * rather than leaving its mark to speak for itself.
 */
import RuntimeMarks from '../components/RuntimeMarks.astro'
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets'
import { links } from '../lib/site'
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro'
import runtimesPanel from '../assets/screenshots/runtimes-panel.png'
---

<section id="runtimes" class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head section-head--center">
      <h2 class="d2">Bring the agents you already use. They finally talk to each other.</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Each one is strong on its own and trapped in its own terminal, with its own memory and no
        record of what the others did. Clawboo gives all five one board, one chat and one memory,
        and any of them can lead.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- The marks are the payoff here, not a label, so they get the room. The
         spacing lives on a wrapper this component owns: a class passed into
         RuntimeMarks lands on an element in ITS scope, so a scoped rule here
         would never match it. -->
    <div class="marks-block">
      <RuntimeMarks />
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <div class="panel-grid">
        <div>
          <h3 class="d3">Nothing to install to start</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-secondary">
            Clawboo Native is built in and speaks to eleven providers. Bring a key from any one of
            them and a team is running before you install anything. Claude Code, Codex and Hermes
            take a key or a single sign-in; OpenClaw connects as a Gateway.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-secondary">
            However they join, they coordinate through the board and structured messages, never by
            reading each other&rsquo;s screen output, and each works in its own copy of the code so
            two agents can&rsquo;t overwrite each other.
          </p>
          <a
            class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[15px] font-medium text-brand hover:underline"
            href={links.docs}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            Read the runtimes guide
            <Icon name="arrow-up-right" size={14} />
          </a>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-media">
          <Picture
            src={runtimesPanel}
            alt="The Runtimes panel: Clawboo Native connected, Claude Code and Codex ready to connect, Hermes connected, and an OpenClaw Gateway."
            formats={['avif', 'webp']}
            widths={[720, 1080, 1440, 1920]}
            sizes="(min-width: 900px) 680px, 94vw"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Generous on both sides: the row is the centre of gravity for this section
     now that the cards are gone, and it was the tight margin against the
     heading that made it feel congested when it sat above. */
  .marks-block {
    margin-block: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5.5rem);
  }
</style>
